%[text] %[text:anchor:H_47446215] ## Moment
%[text] The moment measures the rotational force in a system. Moment can also be called torque or moment of force. The radius at which the force acts is called the moment arm. Levers apply the principle of moment to make a small amount of force applied at one end of a moment arm impart significantly more force at the other end.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1D766ADD] ### Moment with respect to a point
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_011121C3] #### $\\vec{M} = \\vec{r}\\times \\vec{F}${"altText":"vector(M) = vector(r) times vector(F)"}
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:7d30) **Try.** What is the moment about point $C${"altText":"C"} when a constant downward force of 15 [Newtons](internal:M_4600CDDF) is applied at point A and a variable force is applied at point B with arms $L\_1 = 1.1 \\text{ m}${"altText":"L\_1 = 1.1 m"} and $L\_2 = 0.1 \\text{ m}${"altText":"L\_2 = 0.1 m"}?
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A line from A to B with a support shown close to B labeled C. The distance from A to C is called L\_1 and the distance from B to C is called L\_2.](text:image:58ce) 
L_1 = 1.1; % m
L_2 = 0.1; % m
F_A = 15;  % N
%[text] Let's define a coordinate system where up is $+z${"altText":"+z"}, right is $+y${"altText":"+y"} and out towards us is $+x${"altText":"+x"}.  
r_1 = [0 L_1 0];
FAvec = [0 0 -F_A];
%[text] Then the moment about C caused by the force at A is:
M_AC = cross(r_1,FAvec)
%[text] **Note:** In 2D beam analysis, a positive moment is said to be in a counter-clockwise direction because of the rotation it will impart. A negative moment is said to be in a clockwise direction. 
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:91a4) **Reflect**. What is the direction (positive/counter-clockwise or negative/clockwise) of the moment `M_AC` about which axis?
checkDir([-1 0 0]) %[control:dropdown:088c]{"position":[1,19]}
%%
%[text] Now compute the moment at C caused by the force you chose at B:
F_B = 0; % N %[control:slider:71d3]{"position":[7,8]}
r_2 = [0 -L_2 0];
FBvec = [0 0 -F_B];
M_BC = cross(r_2,FBvec)
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:6e07) **Reflect**. What value of `FBvec` results in a static equilibrium for this system? Why? 
%[text] Run this section to check whether the values you have chosen for `L_1`, `L_2`, `F_A`, and `F_B` balance.
checkStaticF_B(FAvec,r_1,FBvec,r_2)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_751CB7ED] ### Moment along an axis
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_3A0DF0D2] #### $M\_i = (\\vec{r}\\times \\vec{F})\\cdot \\vec{e}\_i${"altText":"M\_i = (vector(r) times vector(F)) dot vector(e)\_i"}
%[text] While the moment with respect to a point is a vector perpendicular to the force and the moment arm, the moment along an axis is a scalar value. The calculation of the moment along an axis requires defining both a point and an axis. The [unit vector](internal:M_1DD1BA81) $\\vec{e}\_i${"altText":"vector(e)\_i"} is along the axis and the vector $\\vec{r}${"altText":"vector(r)"} is from a point on the axis to the point of application of the force $\\vec{F}${"altText":"vector(F)"}. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FF35E456] ### ![](text:image:39e2) **Exercise 1: Calculate the Moment of Force Around an Axis**
%[text] In the following figure, the magnitude of $F\_B=858 \\text{ N}${"altText":"F\_B=858 N"} and the magnitude of $F\_C=462 \\text{ N}${"altText":"F\_C=462 N"}. The measured distances are $d\_1 = 6 \\text{ m}${"altText":"d\_1 = 6 m"}, $d\_2 = 2\\text{ m}${"altText":"d\_2 = 2 m"}, $d\_3 = 3\\text{ m}${"altText":"d\_3 = 3 m"}, and $d\_4 = 2.5\\text{ m}${"altText":"d\_4 = 2.5 m"}. Determine the resulting moment of force with respect to an axis parallel to the direction of the $x${"altText":"x"}-axis around the point $O${"altText":"O"}. Repeat this calculation to determine the moment of force parallel to the $y${"altText":"y"}-axis. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A flagpole is located along the positive Z-axis with positive X pointing left and positive Y pointing right. A is the top of the flag pole at a height d\_1. C is the left support located a distance d\_2 in the positive X-direction and d\_3 in the negative Y direction. Point B is the right support located on the positive Y axis at a distance d\_4. F\_C points from A to C and F\_B points from A to B.](text:image:4eab)
%[text] Identify the Cartesian coordinates of each point in the diagram. This is an exercise in reading the diagram, no calculation is required.
O = [0 0 0];
A = nan; %[control:editfield:5b4d]{"position":[5,8]}
B = nan; %[control:editfield:66b6]{"position":[5,8]}
C = nan; %[control:editfield:601f]{"position":[5,8]}
CheckEx1Coords(A,B,C)
%%
%[text] Create the [unit vectors](internal:M_1DD1BA81) $u\_{AC}${"altText":"u\_AC"} in the $\\vec{AC}${"altText":"vector(AC)"} direction and $u\_{AB}${"altText":"u\_AB"} in the $\\vec{AB}${"altText":"vector(AB)"} direction. 
%[text] **Hint:** Remember the definitions of unit vectors discussed in [StaticForces.m](file:./StaticForces.m:M_A3BAD560). 
u_AC = nan; %[control:editfield:659e]{"position":[8,11]}
u_AB = nan; %[control:editfield:3b25]{"position":[8,11]}
%[text] Define the vector forces $\\vec{F}\_{C}${"altText":"vector(F)\_C"} and $\\vec{F}\_B${"altText":"vector(F)\_B"}:
F_C = nan; %[control:editfield:1360]{"position":[7,10]}
F_B = nan; %[control:editfield:8fdd]{"position":[7,10]}
%[text] Define the [resultant force](internal:M_7B183274):
F_R = nan % N-m %[control:editfield:0247]{"position":[7,10]}
%[text] Calculate the moment on the flag pole with respect to the point $O${"altText":"O"}. You will want to use the command [`cross`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/cross.html)`.`
r_OA = A-O;
MR = nan % N-m %[control:editfield:8ed4]{"position":[6,9]}
%[text] Calculate the moment with respect to the $x${"altText":"x"} and $y${"altText":"y"} axes. What do you 
e_x = [1 0 0];
e_y = [0 1 0];

Mx = nan % N-m %[control:editfield:7209]{"position":[6,9]}
My = nan % N-m %[control:editfield:8e71]{"position":[6,9]}

  %[control:button:563e]{"position":[1,2]}
out1 = CheckEx1(Mx,My,F_C,F_B,r_OA);
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:25f0) **Reflect**. Given these results for the moment on the flag pole, is it falling over? Why or why not?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4B30B12F] ### ![](text:image:9f89) **Exercise 2: Recalculate the Moment of Force Around a Different Axis**
%[text] Now recalculate for the moment with respect to an axis passing through $O${"altText":"O"} in the $XY${"altText":"XY"} plane at an angle of $45^{\\circ}${"altText":"45^degrees"}.
M_axis = nan %[control:editfield:881d]{"position":[10,13]}
if ~exist("out1","var") || out1==-1
    disp("Please complete Exercise 1 before attempting Exercise 2.")
else
    CheckEx2(M_axis);
end
  %[control:button:9f26]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:1ee1) **Reflect**. 
%[text] 1. Compare the results of Exercise 1 and Exercise 2. Do they make sense? Why or why not?
%[text] 2. If we choose a different coordinate system to work in, does it change the physics of the problem?
%[text] 3. What does it mean to have a reaction moment (or a reaction force) that acts at zero distance from the point of reference? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A693EB52] ### ![](text:image:0470) **Exercise 3: Determine the Maximum Load with Fixed Maximal Tensions**
%[text] Three cables come together at a hook $A${"altText":"A"} and terminate at points $B${"altText":"B"}, $C${"altText":"C"}, and $D${"altText":"D"} along the edges of a uniform sheet of material with dimensions provided. Let `w` be the weight of the sheet in [Newtons](internal:M_4600CDDF) and `g` be the acceleration due to gravity in $\\frac{m}{s^2}${"altText":"(m)\/(s^2)"}.
g = 9.81;    % m/s^2
%[text] Determine the maximum mass in kilograms that can be supported in this fashion without exceeding 15kN in any cable.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A horizontal sheet in the X-Y plane is supported from a single point, A, by three cables connected to B on the left, C on the back, and D on the right. B is a distance d\_1 from the front of the sheet. The X-axis passes through a distance d\_2 from the left and d\_3 from the right of the sheet. The Y-axis is a distance d\_4 from the front of the sheet. The point D is on the right edge a distance d\_8 from the Y-axis towards the rear of the sheet, and from D to the rear is d\_9. The supporting point A is at a height of d\_5. Point C is a distance d\_6 from the left edge and d\_7 from the right edge.](text:image:2162)
% Set up the distances
d_1 = 2;  % m
d_2 = 7;  % m
d_3 = 7;  % m
d_4 = 6;  % m
d_5 = 12; % m
d_6 = 2;  % m
d_7 = 10; % m
d_8 = 4;  % m
d_9 = 1;  % m

% Compute the mass here
%[text] First, define the points `A`, `B`, `C`, and `D`. You will also need to define the unit vectors in each relevant direction.























%[text] Define the symbolic tension in each cable: $T\_{AB}${"altText":"T\_AB"}, $T\_{AC}${"altText":"T\_AC"} and $T\_{AD}${"altText":"T\_AD"}. 
syms TAB TAC TAD  % N




%[text] Define a symbolic unknown to represent the weight we are trying to solve for. 
syms w    % N
%[text] Add up all the forces in each direction: `F_`x, `F_`y, `F_`z. 
F_x = 0 == 0;
F_y = 0 == 0;
F_z = 0 == 0;
%[text] Then use [`solve`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/sym.solve.html) to find the values of `TAB`, `TAC`, and `TAD` in terms of `w`.
[TAB, TAC, TAD] = solve([F_x F_y F_z],[TAB TAC TAD])
%[text] Use `solve` again to determine the value of `w` that will result in a force of 15kN in each cable.



%[text] What is the maximum mass in kilograms that can be supported in this fashion without exceeding 15kN in any cable?
mass =  0; %[control:editfield:47b3]{"position":[8,10]}
CheckEx3(mass,d_1,d_2,d_3,d_4,d_5,d_6,d_7,d_8,d_9);
